\end{table}

\section{Recommendations}
\begin{enumerate}
	\item \textbf{Adopt for Development Environments}: WAuth is ideal for local development where machine-locking provides natural isolation between environments.
	\item \textbf{Use Custom Keys for Production}: For production deployments, explicitly specified custom keys ensure portability.
	\item \textbf{Implement Regular Key Rotation}: Utilize the built-in key rotation feature every 90 days.
	\item \textbf{Enable TTL for Temporary Secrets}: Set appropriate TTL values to auto-expire session tokens and temporary credentials.
\end{enumerate}

% =============================================================================
% CHAPTER 2: INTRODUCTION
% =============================================================================
\chapter{Introduction}
\cleardoublepage

\section{Background}
Secret management represents one of the most challenging aspects of secure software development. Developers frequently need to store API keys, database credentials, certificates, and other sensitive configuration values. Traditional approaches such as storing secrets in environment variables or configuration files present significant security risks:

\begin{itemize}
	\item Environment variables can be accidentally exposed in logs or error messages
	\item Configuration files may be committed to version control
	\item Secrets remain valid indefinitely unless manually rotated
	\item No mechanism for automatic expiration exists
\end{itemize}

WAuth addresses these challenges by providing a machine-locked encrypted storage system that:

\begin{enumerate}
	\item \textbf{Encrypts all stored secrets} using industry-standard Fernet encryption
	\item \textbf{Ties encryption to the machine} where secrets were created
	\item \textbf{Supports automatic expiration} through Time-To-Live (TTL) features
	\item \textbf{Provides key rotation} capabilities for regular security updates
	\item \textbf{Enables backup and restore} for disaster recovery
	\item \textbf{Integrates with Docker secrets} for containerized deployments
\end{enumerate}

\section{Objectives}
This technical whitepaper aims to:

\begin{enumerate}
	\item \textbf{Document the system architecture} and design decisions
	\item \textbf{Analyze the cryptographic implementation} and security properties
	\item \textbf{Evaluate code quality} and maintainability metrics
	\item \textbf{Assess technical debt} and identify areas for improvement
	\item \textbf{Provide deployment guidance} and best practices
	\item \textbf{Generate risk assessment} for enterprise adoption
\end{enumerate}

\section{Scope}
This document covers:

\begin{itemize}
	\item Complete codebase analysis including wauth/, test/, and examples/
	\item All 11 core modules: core.py, vault.py, utils.py, exceptions.py, drivers/, etc.
	\item CI/CD pipeline and documentation infrastructure
	\item Security audit results and recommendations
	\item Performance benchmarks and stress projections
\end{itemize}

